import json,os,numpy as np
from fit_models import specs
R=json.load(open('final_results.json'))
reference="Background: B. Armstrong-Helouvry, P. Dupont and C. Canudas de Wit, 'A survey of models, analysis tools and compensation methods for the control of machines with friction', Automatica (1994). This bibliographic reference is recalled from memory; the attempted web retrieval did not return usable source text. The custom algebraic closures here are not claimed to be the exact models in that survey."
D='data/train.csv, all 13 trajectories, and data/val.csv, all 3 trajectories. The validation file was first reserved for comparison of training-only structures, then included in the final estimation to reduce parameter uncertainty. The final loss uses every angle sample after the first of each trajectory, with equal weight per sample; the first logged `theta` and `omega` initialize that trajectory. Inputs `u` are held at the logged value between samples. No test data were accessed. In cross-validation the three validation trajectories are internally numbered 14 through 16; their file IDs remain 1 through 3.'
units={'a':'rad/(s^2*V)','b':'1/s','g':'rad/s^2','c':'rad/s^2','k':'1/s','hs':'-','hc':'-','eta':'-','v':'rad/s','vs':'rad/s'}
desc={'a':'Voltage-to-acceleration gain; positive motor torque per effective inertia.','b':'Viscous acceleration coefficient, including mechanical drag and motor back-EMF effects that cannot be separated by these data.','g':'Sinusoidal restoring-acceleration amplitude, interpreted as gravitational torque divided by effective inertia.','c':'Dry-friction acceleration scale.','k':'Low-speed stopping relaxation rate used by the algebraic friction regularization, not a separately measured material property.','hs':'Dimensionless sine coefficient of angular friction modulation.','hc':'Dimensionless cosine coefficient of angular friction modulation.','eta':'Dimensionless directional asymmetry of positive and negative sliding friction limits.','v':'Low-speed regularization width of the friction transition.','vs':'Speed scale of the Gaussian speed-weakening friction envelope.'}
f='a*u-g*sin(theta)';ct='c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))';blend='exp(-(omega/v)**2)';dead=f'Max({f}-{ct},0)+Min({f}+{ct},0)'
models={
'AngleClip':('angle_asym',['a','b','g','c','k','hs','hc','eta'],f'{f}-b*omega-Min(Max({f}+k*omega,-{ct}*(1-eta)),{ct}*(1+eta))'),
'SmoothAngle':('angle_smooth',['a','b','g','c','v','hs','hc'],f'{f}-b*omega-{ct}*tanh(omega/v)'),
'StaticBlend':('equal_blend',['a','b','g','c','k','hs','hc','v'],f'{blend}*({dead})+(1-{blend})*({f}-{ct}*tanh(omega/v))-b*omega-k*{blend}*omega'),
'SpeedWeakening':('speed_weakening',['a','b','g','c','v','vs'],'a*u-g*sin(theta)-b*omega-c*exp(-(omega/vs)**2)*tanh(omega/v)')}
texts={
'AngleClip':{
 'family':'Second-order nonlinear pendulum torque balance with an algebraic projected Coulomb/stiction closure and a trigonometric friction envelope. '+reference,
 'interpretation':'`dtheta = omega` is the exact kinematic identity. `a*u` is motor acceleration, `-g*sin(theta)` is restoring acceleration, and `-b*omega` is viscous drag. The friction magnitude `c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))` varies periodically with angle; this is an empirical positive envelope, not an identified pad-pressure law. Its positive-direction limit is multiplied by `1+eta` and its negative-direction limit by `1-eta`. The clipped term `Min(Max(a*u-g*sin(theta)+k*omega,-c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))*(1-eta)),c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))*(1+eta))` opposes motion when saturated. When unsaturated it cancels the external torque and leaves acceleration `-(b+k)*omega`. At `omega = 0` it supports a whole stick band rather than causing slow creep. `k` controls the numerically resolved stopping relaxation, not a new state.',
 'origin':'The motor, viscous and sine terms came from torque-balance physics. Coulomb friction was first diagnosed by ordinary least squares on moving training samples. A force projection was chosen instead of an abrupt velocity sign switch to maintain a stick band with stable fixed-step RK4 integration. The expanded moving-sample linear-library residual fit supported angular friction terms `-sign(omega)*sin(theta)` and `-sign(omega)*cos(theta)`; these motivated the positive periodic friction envelope. Directional mean residuals motivated separate positive and negative limits. A fitted restoring harmonic, quadratic drag and cubic voltage gain did not produce a convincing improvement and were not retained. This expression was designed from those diagnostics, not produced by genetic programming.',
 'limitations':'Torque and inertia are identified only through their ratios. The angular envelope and directional asymmetry may include encoder processing, unmodelled loads and run-to-run friction differences; they do not establish a specific physical preload mechanism. The force projection approximates set-valued dry friction with a finite stopping rate, so it is not an exact event-driven Coulomb solver. Stick/slip transition derivatives are not smooth. Training voltages actually cover roughly `-4.95 <= u <= 3.96` V, much less than the formal `-10 <= u <= 10` V domain; linear motor behavior outside the observed range is assumed. Several stopping-angle and noisy driven-trajectory residuals remain. No hidden-state hysteresis, motor-current dynamics, drift or temperature dependence is identifiable here.'},
'SmoothAngle':{
 'family':'Regularized Coulomb-plus-viscous pendulum model with an empirical trigonometric friction envelope. '+reference,
 'interpretation':'`dtheta = omega` is kinematics. `a*u`, `-g*sin(theta)` and `-b*omega` represent motor drive, restoring torque per inertia and viscous drag. `-c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))*tanh(omega/v)` is smooth, odd-in-speed dry-friction drag, with an angle-dependent positive magnitude. `hs` and `hc` describe mathematical modulation rather than a measured pressure law. `v` makes friction approximately linear at low speed and Coulomb-like outside that region.',
 'origin':'This is the smooth Coulomb benchmark from the preregistered physical model plan, extended with angular friction modulation after a moving-sample linear-library residual fit supported sine and cosine modulation of sliding friction. The sine restoring torque was retained because the linear restoring law gave substantially larger derivative error. The structure was specified manually; only its constants were optimized. Robust estimation was chosen over ordinary output-error least squares by leave-one-trajectory-out simulation comparison.',
 'limitations':'Unlike the recommended projection model, `tanh(omega/v)` supplies zero friction at exactly zero speed: the only exact free equilibria are zeros of the sine restoring torque. Apparent sticking is low-speed creep, so it cannot reproduce permanent nonzero rest angles. `v` reaches its chosen lower bound, indicating insufficient evidence to estimate a sharp physical transition width. The regularization is also sampling-period sensitive. The periodic envelope is empirical and may absorb sensor artifacts. Motor torque and inertia cannot be separated, and voltage extrapolation beyond the observed range is an assumption.'},
'StaticBlend':{
 'family':'Continuous static/kinetic friction blending model with a force dead zone, a Gaussian velocity blending function and a nonlinear pendulum torque balance. '+reference,
 'interpretation':'`dtheta = omega` is kinematics. Define the external acceleration as `a*u-g*sin(theta)`, the angular dry-friction scale as `c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))`, and the near-rest weight as `exp(-(omega/v)**2)`. The dead-zone term `Max(a*u-g*sin(theta)-c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta)),0)+Min(a*u-g*sin(theta)+c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta)),0)` transmits only torque beyond the friction threshold. Its weight tends to one at rest. The kinetic term `(1-exp(-(omega/v)**2))*(a*u-g*sin(theta)-c*(1+hs*sin(theta)+hc*cos(theta))*tanh(omega/v))` takes over with increasing speed. `-b*omega` is viscous drag; `-k*exp(-(omega/v)**2)*omega` damps the near-rest motion. Static and kinetic friction scales are assumed equal. Angular modulation is empirical.',
 'origin':'The dead zone came from the need to reproduce nonzero sticking angles. A Gaussian velocity blend was a deliberate smooth-closure design choice, not a symbolic-search discovery. Angular modulation was added after moving-sample library diagnostics, as for the projection model. An unconstrained version fitted a static limit below the sliding scale, which was not physically convincing. An additional nonnegative static-friction excess was then tested and fitted essentially to zero on the training data; it was removed. The delivered model imposes equal static and kinetic scales and refits all remaining parameters. Robust loss was selected by leave-one-trajectory-out output-error comparison.',
 'limitations':'This closure is a smooth algebraic approximation, not a hybrid model with exact slip events or a presliding displacement state. Its finite velocity transition and additional low-speed damping can affect breakaway and small perturbations of static equilibria. The data do not determine a separate static excess, so equality of static and kinetic scales is an explicit assumption. Directional friction and hidden motor-current dynamics are not included. The angle envelope is empirical, and extrapolation in voltage is unverified.'},
'SpeedWeakening':{
 'family':'Gaussian Stribeck-type speed-weakening friction with viscous drag and a pendulum torque balance. '+reference,
 'interpretation':'`dtheta = omega` is kinematics. `a*u` is motor acceleration, `-g*sin(theta)` is restoring acceleration and `-b*omega` is viscous drag. `-c*exp(-(omega/vs)**2)*tanh(omega/v)` opposes angular velocity, approaching smooth Coulomb drag at speeds above `v` but below `vs`, and decreasing at higher speeds. `c` controls the friction scale, `v` the near-zero regularization and `vs` the speed-weakening scale. There is no angle-dependent friction in this candidate.',
 'origin':'The original physically motivated smooth Coulomb family was extended by a standard Gaussian Stribeck envelope after speed-dependent derivative residuals were examined. In its initial fit a separate high-speed dry-friction asymptote converged essentially to zero, while the viscous term increased. The asymptote was therefore removed rather than retained as a tiny uninterpretable coefficient; all six remaining parameters were re-estimated jointly. This is an independently defensible alternative torque-balance family, not a perturbation of the projection coefficients. Its robust-loss fit was selected by cross-validation.',
 'limitations':'The high-speed dry-friction asymptote is assumed zero; these recordings do not determine it separately from viscous drag. This is an effective speed-weakening approximation, not evidence that felt friction vanishes at arbitrarily high speed. It has no exact nonzero stick band: at rest the friction term is zero, so nonzero resting angles slowly creep. The fitted transition width reaches its imposed lower bound. It omits the angular and directional effects that improved the recommended model. Its overall cross-validated angle error is larger, but it is useful as a structurally simpler speed-dependent alternative.'}}

os.makedirs('models',exist_ok=True)
for label,(name,keys,expr) in models.items():
 rec=R[name]['selected'];p=rec['p'];lb,ub=rec['bounds'];pars=[]
 for key,val in zip(keys,p):
  pars.append({'name':key,'value':float(val),'unit':units[key],'type':'fitted','description':desc[key]+' Estimated jointly with all other parameters of this candidate by the output-error procedure below.'})
 names=', '.join('`'+k+'`'+(' ('+units[k]+')' if units[k]!='-' else ' (dimensionless)') for k in keys)
 def nums(arr):return '['+', '.join(format(float(x),'.17g') for x in arr)+']'
 starts='; '.join('`'+nums(x)+'`' for x in rec['starts'])
 loss=rec['loss']
 crit=('Sum of squared open-loop angle residuals, with residual defined as simulated `theta` minus measured `theta`, on all samples except trajectory initial samples.' if loss=='linear' else 'SciPy soft-L1 loss on open-loop angle residuals, defined as simulated `theta` minus measured `theta`, with loss scale `0.04` rad. This downweights large encoder/transient/stopping discrepancies without discarding their samples.')+' The angle output-error criterion matches the intended autonomous simulation use; differentiation-based acceleration residuals were only used for initialization and structural diagnostics.'
 method=f'All {len(keys)} fitted parameters were optimized together by bounded nonlinear least squares using scipy.optimize.least_squares (Trust Region Reflective algorithm), with `{loss}` loss, `x_scale = "jac"`, `diff_step = 0.0001`, `max_nfev = 350`, `ftol = 2e-8`, `xtol = 2e-8`, and `gtol = 1e-7`. Here solver tolerances and finite-difference scale are numerical settings, not physical parameters. Each residual evaluation integrates separate trajectories by classical RK4, one step of `0.01` s, with zero-order-held `u`, exactly as the task specifies. Numba compiles the simulator; it does not change the model. Parameter ordering is {names}. Six initial vectors in that ordering were {starts}; each component has the corresponding unit just listed. Bounds in that same order are lower `'+nums(lb)+'` and upper `'+nums(ub)+'`, with the corresponding component units. The first start came from the preceding torque-balance/output-error fit; the other five are deterministic local perturbations, using numpy.random.default_rng with seed `271828`, to reduce sensitivity to local minima. The same script evaluates both ordinary squared loss and soft-L1 loss (scale `0.04` rad), with six starts per loss; the best fitting objective is retained within each loss. Leave-one-trajectory-out refits on the 16 available trajectories then select the loss by aggregate held-out angle RMSE. Those refits are warm-started from the full-data estimate but exclude the held-out trajectory from their objective; their limit is `max_nfev = 180`. The delivered values are the selected full-data fit, not an average of cross-validation parameters. Every other setting is identical between those refits and the full-data fit. Positive gain, restoring amplitude and friction scale, nonnegative viscous drag, and bounded modulation coefficients were imposed for stability and the physical requirements. The velocity-width lower bound `0.12` rad/s avoids an excessively stiff explicit-RK4 regularization; stopping rates are bounded below the approximately `278` 1/s scalar RK4 stability limit for this sampling period. Bounds on `hs` and `hc` guarantee a positive angular envelope even at their extreme combinations. This direct low-dimensional output-error method was preferred to a black-box predictor because states are never corrected after initialization.'
 extra=f" Full-data angle RMSE is `{rec['all_rmse']:.8f}` rad and aggregate leave-one-trajectory-out angle RMSE is `{rec['cv_rmse']:.8f}` rad across all 16 known trajectories; these are not test scores."
 texts[label]['limitations']+=extra
 obj={'expressions':{'dtheta':'omega','domega':expr},'parameters':pars,'estimation':{'criterion':crit,'data':D,'method':method,'script':'fit_final.py'},'discussion':texts[label]}
 json.dump(obj,open('models/'+label+'.json','w'),indent=2)
pool={'order':['AngleClip','StaticBlend','SmoothAngle','SpeedWeakening'],'recommended':'AngleClip','reason':'AngleClip had the smallest aggregate leave-one-trajectory-out angle RMSE (`0.06048971` rad) among the retained final families on the 16 known trajectories, compared with `0.06128174` rad for SmoothAngle, `0.06320398` rad for StaticBlend and `0.07691553` rad for SpeedWeakening. The small gap to SmoothAngle alone is not decisive, but AngleClip also preserves a genuine nonzero static stick band, has positive dissipative friction limits, and uses a stopping rate compatible with the required fixed-step RK4 integration. The remaining free-response stopping-angle and driven residuals mean this is a recommendation under uncertainty, not a claim about unavailable test performance.'}
json.dump(pool,open('models/pool.json','w'),indent=2)
